# Soldier — a heavy-set enlisted grunt, authored from a DESCRIPTION (the
# treatment arm of the A/B against mesh-fitting; see commit "plan: author the
# soldier from a DESCRIPTION"). The reference plate
# (docs/dev-notes/refs/soldier-views-ref.png) was read once for the brief and
# put down: nothing below is fit to it. The mesh in refs/soldier-mesh/ was
# used ONLY to bake the face decal, which is what blob:face-bake is for.
#
# THE READ, crown to boots, in three beats: a FLAT GREEN TOP, then a HEAVY
# SHOULDER YOKE, then a WIDE BELTED WAIST. Everything else is a supporting
# mass. He is the ordinary human in a bestiary of monsters (goblin, clown,
# zombie), so he is deliberately the PLAINest member of the cast — but plain
# in the house idiom: few large smooth masses, bold colour blocking doing the
# identifying work (grey-green plate, red chest and boots, olive trousers,
# green hair), two or three exaggerated features (the flat-top, the
# pauldrons, the belt) and no fine detail. Nothing here has a groove, a
# pleat, or a painted seam, because none of the approved three do.
#
# Cast check: the goblin carries its whole character in ears+nose+lips over a
# smooth pot-bellied body; the clown in hat+nose+shoes. The soldier's
# equivalents are flat-top+pauldrons+belt. Same primitive count, same blend
# weights, same "one colour per garment" rule.

# 2026-09-06 silhouette pass: broad barrel torso and neck; all joint centers
# and bone lengths are unchanged so weapon IK keeps its existing reach.
model soldier
  height 1.70
  # humanoid | digitigrade. Checked, not decorative — see blob-checks.ts.
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # Root at 0.87 so the leg chain (0.42 + 0.42 below the pelvis tail at 0.97)
  # lands the ankle at 0.13 — legs are exactly half the standing height,
  # which is what keeps him an ordinary human next to the goblin's stumps
  # and the zombie's stoop.
  root pelvis at 0.87 len=0.10

  # Upright, no hunch — the zombie owns the stoop in this cast. The tiniest
  # backward lean (-1 deg) keeps the chest plate from tipping the silhouette
  # forward in profile.
  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=-1 len=0.16
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=-1 len=0.17
  bone spine2 parent=chest  dir=up pitch=0  len=0.06
  bone neck   parent=spine2 dir=up pitch=0  len=0.08
  # Carries the face block: at=0.55 puts the cranium centre at 1.561, chin
  # ~1.48, flesh crown ~1.647; the hair cap tops at 1.69. Head is 1/8 of
  # 1.70 as briefed.
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=2  len=0.22

  mirror
    # Shoulder socket at x 0.14, y 1.36 — the goblin lesson applies: shoulder
    # reach is a function of chest width. The chest flesh here is half-width
    # ~0.115, so a 0.04/0.10 clavicle lands the socket just proud of it.
    bone clavicle parent=spine2  side=0.040 dir=side  len=0.100
    # Arms hang DOWN and slightly OUT (tilt 8) so the bare arm clears the
    # belt slab; the schoolgirl measured the same fog line (<=6 deg melts
    # arm and waist fields together).
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down tilt=8 pitch=2  len=0.26
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down tilt=4 pitch=8  len=0.24
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down pitch=6         len=0.095
    # Legs: dead straight with a whisper of outward slant, the "at ease"
    # stand. No bow — the goblin owns the bow.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis dir=down tilt=2 side=0.068 len=0.42
    bone shin     parent=thigh  dir=down tilt=2            len=0.42
    # The foot bone only positions the boot prims (the house pattern).
    bone foot     parent=shin   dir=fwd                    len=0.13
  end

body
  # ============================================================
  # TORSO — flesh under armour. Almost none of this is visible: the cuirass,
  # belt and trousers cover it. It exists to fuse limbs to trunk.
  # ============================================================
  # Hips: trousered (olive). This is the TOP of the camo fatigues.
  # BARE TORSO (owner, 2026-09-04: "remove his shirt — all clothing should be
  # mesh more or less"). These three masses were painted olive/dark as a
  # sleeveless top; unpainted they take the palette's skin, so the shirt, the
  # belt, its pouches and its buckle all leave the .blob and become the kit's
  # job. What is left here is body.
  # THE HIPS WEAR THE TROUSERS (owner, 2026-09-05: "fix the hole in his pants
  # so his crotch isn't showing flesh"). This mass lost its paint when the
  # shirt came off, and unpainted means flesh -- so bare skin showed at the
  # crotch between two olive thighs. Same hex as the thighs, so the trouser
  # line runs unbroken from waist to knee. The chest and belly above stay bare.
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.40 r=0.142 wide=1.05 tall=0.90 deep=0.84 blend=0.010 color=5c5d41
  # Waist: trouser top rising to the belt line.
  bar  torso on spine1 from=0.40 to=1.00 r=0.129 r2=0.135 deep=0.85 blend=0.008
  # Chest: the sleeveless top under the plate — only a dark crescent shows
  # at the armpits and midriff, which is the point: bare arms and dark vest
  # against hard plate is most of the read.
  bar  torso on chest  from=0.20 to=0.95 r=0.133 r2=0.145 deep=0.84 blend=0.008

  # ============================================================
  # THE CUIRASS — machined plate, proud of the torso. box + metal exist for
  # exactly this. Grey-green, flat-fronted, hard-edged.
  # ============================================================
  # NO `metal` on the big plates: metal shades as a diffuse floor plus
  # tinted specular, and under the lab's single key that reads BLACK from
  # most yaws (first shoot, 2026-09-04). Painted + moderate gloss keeps the
  # machined edge (box, tight round) and lets the grey-green show. metal
  # stays only on small trim (buckle), where dark is fine.
  # tall 0.70 / at 0.60: the cuirass ENDS above the belt with a band of dark
  # vest between (the reference's midriff break), and its top stays below
  # the pauldron bar so yoke and chest read as two masses, not one T.
  # deep 0.72, not 0.55: at 0.55 the profile torso was paper-thin under a
  # yoke that wide — a barrel chest carries the plate, a plank does not.
  # The muted-red front plate inset, one size smaller and standing a clear
  # centimetre prouder than the cuirass face (v1 sat 4 mm INSIDE it and
  # vanished). The reference's single loudest colour decision, kept.

  # ============================================================
  # THE BELT — the third beat of the silhouette. A wide dark slab breaking
  # the torso's line, plus bold pouches. Pouches are BOXES, not blobs:
  # issued webbing is rectangular.
  # ============================================================
  # Pouch pair, front-left and front-right of the buckle.
  # Centre buckle — one small metal box.

  # ============================================================
  # PAULDRONS — the second beat. One big flat plate per shoulder, sitting
  # ON TOP of the deltoid (offset y +0.03) and spreading wider than the arm
  # socket, so the yoke reads from the front as a single hard bar across
  # the shoulders.
  # ============================================================

  # ============================================================
  # ARMS — BARE, thick, skin. The deltoid must show below the pauldron's
  # lower edge and the forearm must be visibly fatter than a shirted wrist.
  # Unpainted = flesh, so the palette's skin carries them.
  # ============================================================
  # THICKER ARMS (owner, 2026-09-04: "more muscular"). Deltoid 0.060 -> 0.072,
  # upper arm 0.048/0.042 -> 0.060/0.048, forearm cap 0.042 -> 0.050 and the
  # shaft 0.044/0.038 -> 0.054/0.042. The taper is kept STEEPER on the upper
  # arm than before (0.060 -> 0.048 rather than 0.048 -> 0.042) so the mass
  # reads as a deltoid falling into a narrower elbow rather than a uniform
  # tube -- a thicker cylinder alone reads as fat, not muscle.
  blob arm on upperarm at=0.06 r=0.098 tall=1.05 blend=0.010 mirror
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.12 to=0.95 r=0.086 r2=0.058 blend=0.008 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=0.00 r=0.064 blend=0.006 mirror
  # The issued-grunt forearm: fatter than the upper arm's lower end.
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.072 r2=0.048 blend=0.008 mirror
  # Fist — a blunt mitt, no fingers (house rule: the goblin's grabby hand is
  # the only articulated one in the cast).
  # ORB HANDS (owner, 2026-09-05). Deliberately a SPHERE -- wide/tall/deep all
  # 1.00, where this was a flattened 0.90/1.00/1.20 paddle. A paddle at the end
  # of a hanging arm reads as a smear at crotch height; an orb reads as a fist,
  # which is also what the shotgun will need something to grip with. Slightly
  # larger (0.044 -> 0.052) to carry against the bulked forearm, and blended
  # tighter so the wrist stays a wrist.
  blob arm on hand at=0.55 offset=(0,0,0.008) r=0.052 wide=1.00 tall=1.00 deep=1.00 blend=0.005 mirror

  # ============================================================
  # LEGS — camo fatigues. Camo is done the house way: TWO bold olive blocks
  # (thigh / shin), not pattern noise. Knee plates and boots break the leg
  # into plate / cloth / plate.
  # ============================================================
  # A BARE FLESH FOOT, because removing the boots removed his feet entirely and
  # left the legs ending at the ankle. Feet are body; boots are the kit's, and
  # they go OVER this. Rounded rather than boxed -- a bare foot has no machined
  # edges, and the boot that wraps it will supply the hard silhouette.
  # NO FLESH FOOT (owner, 2026-09-05: "they still leak out the sides... is
  # there a way to just not render them when wearing shoes"). The boot IS the
  # foot: the kit's foot shell stands on y=0 and swallows the shaft, and the
  # flesh blob under it only ever showed as leaks at the heel and the sides
  # through every stride. The foot BONE stays -- the boot is skinned to it and
  # the rig's toe point is pinned to the ankle off it (rig-bind.ts pinTips).
  # If he ever goes barefoot, this is the line to restore:
  #   blob leg on foot at=0.40 offset=(0.004,-0.094,0.020) r=0.042 wide=0.95 tall=0.72 deep=1.60 blend=0.008 mirror

  # LEGS ARE BODY NOW. The knee plates, the boots and the boot-shaft paint on
  # the shin all left for the kit (owner, 2026-09-04) -- webbing, plate and
  # footwear are mesh. The trousers stay as PAINT for the moment: they are
  # cloth on the leg rather than a separate object, so they cost nothing here.
  blob leg on thigh at=0.00 r=0.084 deep=1.05 blend=0.008 mirror core color=5c5d41
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.02 to=1.00 r=0.079 r2=0.061 deep=1.05 blend=0.007 mirror color=5c5d41
  # Knee plate — hard box proud of the knee.
  # to 0.68 -> 1.00 (main, 2026-09-05): the shin used to stop at 68% of its
  # bone because the BOOT covered the rest. With no flesh foot at all now, the
  # shin runs to the ankle so the leg has an end inside the boot shaft rather
  # than a blunt stump above it. Tapered harder (r2 0.050 -> 0.044) so the
  # ankle narrows.
  bar  leg on shin from=0.08 to=1.00 r=0.060 r2=0.044 blend=0.006 mirror color=676549

  # ============================================================
  # BOOTS — heavy, muted red (the second red accent), with a darker sole.
  # The boot SHAFT covers the lower shin, so the trouser block above ends
  # at 0.68 and the red starts there.
  # ============================================================
  # Foot mass: heel behind the ankle, toe well forward (the schoolgirl
  # shoe's authored-as-world-displacement pattern).
  # (a box takes no tip= — the foot's length is all in deep= and offset z.)
  # Sole — a flat dark slab under the foot.

  # Neck: skin, thick — a grunt's neck, not the schoolgirl's 0.028.
  bar head on neck from=0.05 to=0.95 r=0.044 deep=1.10 blend=0.004

  # Narrow flat strip along the crown, with parallel sides at the front.
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,0.071,0.010) r=0.070 wide=0.48 tall=0.24 deep=0.52 box round=0.18 blend=0.004 color=2e6034
  # Only the rear tip tapers: a short join tucked into the crown, rather
  # than a broad cap with a long tail running down the back of the head.
  blob head on skull at=0.55 offset=(0,0.068,-0.020) tip=(0,-0.025,-0.043) r=0.028 r2=0.012 wide=1.10 tall=0.42 deep=0.70 blend=0.008 color=2e6034

# ============================================================
# FACE BLOCK — owner-tuned in the lab, 2026-09-06.
# The face itself is the baked texture below;
# three dispatches proved painted-prim features read as a visor band, and no
# eye/mouth prims are added here for that reason.
# ============================================================
face
  headRadius 0.075
  headWidth  0.980
  headHeight 1.150
  headDepth  1.050
  jawWidth   0.816
  jawHeight  0.900
  jawDrop    0.060
  jawJut     0.037
  noseLength 0.031
  noseWidth  1.085
  noseDrop   0.014
  browHeavy  0.014
  browRise   0.035
  headBlend  0.005
  headRise   0
  headLead   0

# Existing face bake with the owner's lab projection and Replace blend.
# The body keeps its lighter skin palette; only painted red eyes emit.
sheet
  image       soldier-face.png
  decal       1
  blendLuma   0
  projScaleX  0.210
  projScaleY  0.210
  projCentreX 0.50
  projCentreY 0.510
  eyeGlowAmp  2.2
  eyeGlowCut  0.55
  eyeGlowRedOnly 1
  texRelief   1.050
  texStrength 1
  projSpherical 0
  faceForward 1

# Light, relatively dry skin separates the face and bare arms from olive
# armor. The game must apply this character palette (not the zombie preset)
# and the sheet's blend settings for the same result as the lab.
palette
  surfaceNoiseAmp 0.06
  silhouetteNoiseAmp 0.016
  baseColor     0.522 0.424 0.333
  deepColor     0.50 0.08 0.08
  charColor     0.20 0.12 0.09
  specIntensity 0.35
  specRoughness 0.5
  fresnelBoost  1.5
  translucency  0.2
  wetness       0.79
  mottleAmp     0.10
  mottleScale   1.4
  mottleColor   0.54 0.39 0.28
